### Pending /teach target — Look-ahead bias, measured

**Date flagged:** 2026-07-07
**Status:** Numbers now exist (not yet taught)

**Context:** `analysis/walkforward.py` + `docs/perf/03-backtest-vs-live.md`
turn "look-ahead bias" from a warning into concrete numbers: on real daily
CL/XLE the backtest-vs-live gap is **zero** (contemporaneous at every window,
`leader_agreement_rate=1.000`), while a synthetic regime-flip control shows the
tool *can* detect divergence (`agreement=0.489`, `lag_drift=2.044`,
`mean_oos_gap=−0.237` — the frozen full-sample lag scores *worse* out-of-sample).

**Concept to teach:** point-in-time / walk-forward evaluation — bias lives in
*parameter instability*, not in the mere use of full-sample data; a null result
is a result; the bias can cut both ways. Good contrast pair: backtest (full
sample visible) vs walk-forward (information-set restricted). Grounded in
`walkforward_ccf`'s strict-slicing guard (the poison-the-future test).
